arm64: dts: qcom: qrb2210-arduino-imola: let the host set the RTC

The RTC in the PM4125 PMIC is never set: the rtc-pm8xxx driver only
writes it with allow-set-time and otherwise needs an offset store, which
rtc@6000 does not have. The RTC counts from zero, so every boot reads
1970-01-14 and CONFIG_RTC_HCTOSYS sets the clock to it.

The offset store in the UEFI variable RTCInfo does not help here: this
firmware keeps variables written at runtime in RAM (RTStorageVolatile is
VolatileVars.bin), so the offset is gone at the next reset. Set
allow-set-time instead, so that CONFIG_RTC_SYSTOHC writes the time to the
PMIC registers after NTP and the next boot reads it back. Whether the
PMIC accepts register writes from the host is not documented; this patch
is how to find out.

Set it on the Arduino board only: pm4125.dtsi is shared with the RB1,
whose firmware is not known to allow it.

Upstream-Status: Pending

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/pm4125.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/pm4125.dtsi
@@ -66,7 +66,7 @@
 			status = "disabled";
 		};

-		rtc@6000 {
+		pm4125_rtc: rtc@6000 {
 			compatible = "qcom,pm8941-rtc";
 			reg = <0x6000>, <0x6100>;
 			reg-names = "rtc", "alarm";
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/qrb2210-arduino-imola-base.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/qrb2210-arduino-imola-base.dts
@@ -315,6 +315,10 @@
 	status = "okay";
 };

+&pm4125_rtc {
+	allow-set-time;
+};
+
 &pm4125_vbus {
 	regulator-min-microamp = <500000>;
 	regulator-max-microamp = <500000>;
